  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on 30-meter resolution satellite data, which may not capture smaller, fragmented patches of urban vegetation that contribute to local cooling.",
      "what": "This study mapped and analyzed the spatial-temporal distribution of urban green spaces and land surface temperatures in Azamgarh, India, using 30-meter resolution Landsat satellite imagery from 2000, 2011, and 2022. The analysis tracked changes in land use, Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI), and Land Surface Temperature (LST) to calculate per capita and per-unit area green space availability. The findings show a continuous decline in green cover over the 22-year period, which directly correlates with rising local surface temperatures and an expansion of the city's high-temperature zones.",
      "why": "It provides empirical evidence of how rapid conversion of vegetation to impervious surfaces accelerates the urban heat island effect in a rapidly growing, medium-sized Indian city. This establishes a direct, localized link between declining per capita green space and rising thermal stress to inform local urban planning interventions."
    }
  },
